UART capture
============

A programming jig can wire a UART on the provisioning host to the serial
console of the board in each head. Mapping the head's USB port to that UART
here makes rpi-sb-uart-capture keep everything the board prints: bootloader,
firmware, kernel and fastbootd messages, fastboot errors among them.

Directories
-----------

    /usr/share/rpi-sb-provisioner/uart.d/    package-supplied mappings
    /etc/rpi-sb-provisioner/uart.d/          local administrator mappings

Files are merged by basename with the /etc copy winning, as in usb-ports.d:
an empty /etc file of the same name masks a package's mapping. Restart the
service after a change:

    sudo systemctl restart rpi-sb-uart-capture

File format
-----------

One mapping per line, in a *.conf file:

    <usb-port> <tty> [baud]

The port is a USB topology path as in usb-ports.d, but exact: no globs, as
one UART belongs to one head. The baud rate defaults to 115200. Blank lines
are ignored, as is anything from a '#' to the end of a line. A port or a tty
may be mapped only once.

    # CM5 programming jig: the head's console is on GPIO 14 and 15.
    4-1 /dev/ttyAMA0

The service is confined to ttyAMA, ttyS, ttyUSB and ttyACM devices. It opens
the UART read-only and never writes to it, as the host's TX reaches the
board's console. Nothing else may use the UART: no login prompt, and no
console= on the host's kernel command line.

Where the output goes
---------------------

    /var/log/rpi-sb-provisioner/uart/<port>.log
        Every line from the port, timestamped in UTC. Rotated to .1 at 8 MiB.

    /var/log/rpi-sb-provisioner/<serial>/uart.log
        The lines of one device: from up to 30 seconds before its bootstrap
        started, to 15 seconds after its provisioner finished or a stage
        aborted, so that the board's reboot is included. Shown on the
        device's page as the UART log.

Escape sequences, carriage returns and other control characters are dropped.
Each device log is rebuilt from the port log when its window opens, so a
restart of the capture service loses nothing captured before it.
